      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A memory leak, you say? Well, I'm not surprised. You see, in the world of embedded systems, memory is like a treasure trove of precious gems, and goblins love to hoard them. But, I digress. To track down the memory leak, you'll want to follow a systematic approach, much like a goblin hunter tracking down a particularly pesky goblin in the depths of the forest.\n\nFirst, enable your system's memory debugging tools. This will give you a better understanding of the memory usage over time. You can use tools like Valgrind, AddressSanitizer, or the built-in memory debugging tools provided by your compiler. These tools will help you identify which functions are allocating and deallocating memory, and where the memory is being leaked.\n\nNext, create a memory leak detector. This can be a simple function that increments a counter every time memory is allocated, and decrements it when memory is deallocated. This will give you a rough estimate of the memory leak. You can then use this counter to identify which functions are causing the leak.\n\nAnother approach is to use a memory profiling tool, such as Memcheck or AddressSanitizer, to analyze the memory usage of your system over time.",
